WEBVTT

00:05.000 --> 00:10.000
I’m going to ask you about these words now.

00:11.000 --> 00:14.000
You’ve mentioned one already. Maorach.

00:15.000 --> 00:17.000
That was just a general name for shellfish?

00:17.000 --> 00:18.000
That’s right.

00:19.000 --> 00:20.000
That was quite commonly used?

00:20.000 --> 00:23.000
Aye, it was that.

00:24.000 --> 00:29.000
That didn’t apply to any specific types of shellfish?

00:29.000 --> 00:35.000
No, no I think all shellfish was known as maorach.

00:36.000 --> 00:39.000
Now, [there are/?] two words I’m quite interested in,

00:39.000 --> 00:41.000
[skelf/?] and [skleef/?].

00:41.000 --> 00:42.000
Skelf?

00:42.000 --> 00:45.000
Are you familiar with both of these words?

00:45.000 --> 00:49.000
Well, a skelf. I’ve heard of a skelf.

00:52.000 --> 00:55.000
It’s only connected with wood as far as I know.

00:56.000 --> 01:01.000
A skelf is just a paring of the wood you see.

01:01.000 --> 01:08.000
Any sharp thing coming against the wood
and you just take a skelf off it, you see.

01:08.000 --> 01:09.000
You haven’t heard of [skleef/?]?

01:09.000 --> 01:10.000
No.

01:12.000 --> 01:17.000
I understand that means a notch in a rock.

01:17.000 --> 01:23.000
[Skleef/?]. I think it possibly comes from the same Gaelic word as ‘skelf’.

01:23.000 --> 01:28.000
… I've never heard it referred to as [skleef/?] at all.

01:28.000 --> 01:31.000
I heard it in Tarbert in Carradale, you know.

01:31.000 --> 01:32.000
I wasn’t sure what it was.

01:32.000 --> 01:34.000
They’re different you see.

01:34.000 --> 01:38.000
There’s more Gaelic up that ways.

01:38.000 --> 01:41.000
Now … 

01:41.000 --> 01:47.000
there’s a word that I’ve heard is used on board the skiffs

01:47.000 --> 01:54.000
when they would be paring wood shavings to kindle a fire.

01:54.000 --> 01:57.000
There’s a name for these shavings.

01:57.000 --> 01:59.000
Aye, what’s this?

02:00.000 --> 02:09.000
Sgla. Aah, aye, it starts with an ‘s’ too.

02:10.000 --> 02:11.000
[Slisheams/?]?

02:11.000 --> 02:14.000
[Sliseag/?], [sliseag/?], [sliseags/?].

02:14.000 --> 02:15.000
That’s right, [sliseags/?].

02:15.000 --> 02:17.000
That’s right, [sliseags/?].

02:20.000 --> 02:22.000
Now … 

02:25.000 --> 02:27.000
did you use the word [spleiter/?]? [Spleiter/?].

02:27.000 --> 02:29.000
Many a time.

02:29.000 --> 02:30.000
What was that?

02:30.000 --> 02:33.000
[XX] went through a tub of water

02:33.000 --> 02:36.000
and went [spleitering/?] up the way.

02:38.000 --> 02:41.000
[XX] is called an awful [spleiter/?].

02:45.000 --> 02:48.000
Now there was a word … 

02:48.000 --> 02:53.000
that was, I think, connected with superstition to a certain extent.

02:53.000 --> 02:59.000
It was used about an unlucky person aboard a boat

02:59.000 --> 03:03.000
that would be called a certain name.

03:03.000 --> 03:06.000
Oh well now, I knew that.

03:06.000 --> 03:09.000
Oh dear goodness.

03:11.000 --> 03:15.000
Oh, I should have come here about 20 year ago. 

03:17.000 --> 03:19.000
Was it a bòcan?

03:19.000 --> 03:23.000
A bòcan - that’s correct. A bòcan.

03:25.000 --> 03:29.000
It was a word that was very commonly used in fishing.

03:29.000 --> 03:32.000
What type of people would it apply to?

03:32.000 --> 03:35.000
Just anyone who was unlucky.

03:36.000 --> 03:40.000
Or else maybe some fella coming off for the night you see

03:41.000 --> 03:44.000
and getting no herring and all the rest getting some.

03:44.000 --> 03:50.000
That’s the word you would say, the expression you would … you're a bòcan.

03:50.000 --> 03:52.000
Don't you come oot to [XX]. 

03:58.000 --> 04:02.000
If I could ask you about some words for fish.

04:02.000 --> 04:06.000
Now, there’s quite a range of words used

04:06.000 --> 04:10.000
for various stages of the saithe and its development.

04:11.000 --> 04:14.000
Can you remember some of these for the smaller stuff?

04:14.000 --> 04:15.000
Cuddies.

04:15.000 --> 04:16.000
Cuddies.

04:20.000 --> 04:21.000
And there’s one for the … 
The glaisean

04:21.000 --> 04:22.000
[Kleshin/?].

04:26.000 --> 04:28.000
And now there’s a word that’s used.

04:30.000 --> 04:33.000
It’s more used in Tarbert. Piuchdaidh.

04:33.000 --> 04:34.000
Piuchdaidh

04:35.000 --> 04:37.000
Now, I thinks that’s …

04:38.000 --> 04:43.000
Piuchdaidh that’s just-  you can say that piuchdaidh is a glaisean.

04:43.000 --> 04:46.000
Now was that used in Campbeltown or Dalintober?

04:46.000 --> 04:49.000
Och aye, aye aye.

04:50.000 --> 04:51.000
The glaisean …

04:52.000 --> 04:55.000
the glaisean was supposed to be

04:57.000 --> 05:00.000
the proper name of it, you see.

05:01.000 --> 05:04.000
They are both Gaelic names.

05:04.000 --> 05:06.000
Are they?

05:06.000 --> 05:11.000
Oh well, I suppose any of them will do.

05:12.000 --> 05:17.000
The biggest stage was - can you remember that? The big … 

05:17.000 --> 05:18.000
The stainler?

05:18.000 --> 05:20.000
The stainler, aye.

05:20.000 --> 05:24.000
Now the spotted dogfish had a name.

05:24.000 --> 05:26.000
The Gaelic name for that was used too.

05:28.000 --> 05:33.000
The spotted dogfish. Och.

05:39.000 --> 05:41.000
Was that the mùrlach?

05:43.000 --> 05:46.000
Aye, it's the mùrlach but is that the spotted dogfish?

05:46.000 --> 05:47.000
Aye.

05:49.000 --> 05:51.000
Aye, the mùrlach.

05:51.000 --> 05:53.000
It’s the one without the [XX].

05:53.000 --> 05:55.000
That's right, aye.

05:55.000 --> 05:57.000
You could scrub your -

05:57.000 --> 06:00.000
scrub the floor with the [XX] back.

06:00.000 --> 06:04.000
It was as rough as a, as a,

06:04.000 --> 06:08.000
it was as rough on the back as a scrubbing brush.

06:09.000 --> 06:13.000
Would you skin it first or just let it dry out?

06:13.000 --> 06:15.000
No, no, just … 

06:15.000 --> 06:17.000
What, do you mean for scrubbing?

06:17.000 --> 06:18.000
Aye

06:18.000 --> 06:22.000
Oh no, just catch the old dead fish.

06:23.000 --> 06:25.000
But what would you dae for eating it?

06:25.000 --> 06:27.000
Eh? We weren’t going to eat it.

06:27.000 --> 06:30.000
It was just there, it would never have been ate. 

06:34.000 --> 06:38.000
[...] dogfish.

06:40.000 --> 06:46.000
Now there was a Gaelic word used there for the gurnard?

06:47.000 --> 06:48.000
The gurnard?

07:02.000 --> 07:04.000
No. I’m beat here.

07:04.000 --> 07:07.000
The oudan, an oudan

07:08.000 --> 07:14.000
That’s correct, that’s correct aye.

07:10.000 --> 07:12.000
Aye, you know the name.

07:17.000 --> 07:19.000
Are you familiar with the word cleek?

07:20.000 --> 07:21.000
Aye.

07:21.000 --> 07:28.000
What senses of the word are you familiar with?

07:28.000 --> 07:29.000
A hook.

07:29.000 --> 07:31.000
Would a hook actually be called a cleek?

07:31.000 --> 07:36.000
Aye, well you see when we used to play the …

07:40.000 --> 07:43.000
play the cleek it was just a round ring,

07:45.000 --> 07:49.000
iron ring and the cleek, the cleek …

07:49.000 --> 07:53.000
it propelled the ring along the street as far as you’d like to go. 

07:56.000 --> 08:03.000
The actual instrument for moving the wheel to go round.

08:03.000 --> 08:05.000
Was there actually a hook on it?

08:05.000 --> 08:09.000
Aye, it is a hook. It’s a solid thing.

08:09.000 --> 08:10.000
It’s actually hooked on to the wheel?

08:10.000 --> 08:12.000
Is it actually hooked onto the wheel?

08:12.000 --> 08:18.000
No, no. It was just, you birl your wheel first.

08:18.000 --> 08:23.000
It's only just a light iron ring

08:23.000 --> 08:26.000
and when you just, when you get it going,

08:26.000 --> 08:28.000
just get your cleek in at the back of it.

08:29.000 --> 08:33.000
Aye, I’m too, I’m too young to know that.

08:33.000 --> 08:36.000
I know about the cleek right enough. [XX]

08:37.000 --> 08:41.000
It used to be, it used to be an annual game.

08:41.000 --> 08:43.000
[...]

08:44.000 --> 08:49.000
You'd have the cleek one time, one year,

08:49.000 --> 08:54.000
one part of the year and you’d have the jarries the other part of the year.

08:54.000 --> 08:55.000
You know the jarries?

08:56.000 --> 08:57.000
Marbles.

08:56.000 --> 08:59.000
That’s right, that’s right.

09:02.000 --> 09:04.000
You had all the old-fashioned names.

09:06.000 --> 09:09.000
Now just two or three more here.

09:12.000 --> 09:13.000
I’ll let you by.

09:15.000 --> 09:17.000
I’ll surely manage to squeeze through if I don’t trample your …

09:23.000 --> 09:25.000
Now I don’t know if this word has come in

09:26.000 --> 09:30.000
more into use recently or whether it was used a lot in your day.

09:30.000 --> 09:34.000
It was the word for the wee scallops - the queen scallops.

09:34.000 --> 09:38.000
The queen - queenies? Aye.

09:38.000 --> 09:40.000
No, it never was used,

09:42.000 --> 09:45.000
would never have been used in my day.

09:47.000 --> 09:50.000
What was it they called them then?

09:54.000 --> 09:55.000
Creachans?

09:58.000 --> 10:02.000
Well, no. No, the creachans is …

10:02.000 --> 10:06.000
the creachans only came into

10:08.000 --> 10:13.000
to my vocabulary about 10 years ago.

10:13.000 --> 10:14.000
I suspected that.

10:14.000 --> 10:17.000
I think that’s more a Tarbert word or a…

10:18.000 --> 10:20.000
when they started fishing for them.

10:20.000 --> 10:22.000
That’s right, aye.

10:24.000 --> 10:26.000
I don’t remember what the…

10:27.000 --> 10:29.000
what we used to call them.

10:29.000 --> 10:33.000
It used to be very thick up at Skippinish Bay there.

10:35.000 --> 10:38.000
Someone was getting the fill of the ring net of them.

10:38.000 --> 10:43.000
And maybe a fella would shout to you ‘What did you get there?’

10:43.000 --> 10:45.000
Instead of…

10:46.000 --> 10:49.000
instead of calling them … what did you call them?

10:49.000 --> 10:50.000
Creachans.

10:50.000 --> 10:52.000
Creachans,  we’d tell them …

10:52.000 --> 10:54.000
It wisnae clams, no?

10:54.000 --> 10:59.000
No, no clams is a bigger shellfish.

10:59.000 --> 11:02.000
Strange the way it goes away out of your mind.

11:02.000 --> 11:05.000
Maybe it will come back to you, later maybe.

11:05.000 --> 11:09.000
Aye, afore you’re halfway out the door there it might, eh.

11:11.000 --> 11:16.000
Now there was a wee creature which resembled the porpoise.

11:17.000 --> 11:19.000
Is there a name in that?

11:22.000 --> 11:26.000
I know what you’re asking but I can’t remember.

11:26.000 --> 11:27.000
You just …

11:27.000 --> 11:28.000
Is it a pulag?

11:28.000 --> 11:30.000
Pollag! Pollag! The pollag!

11:30.000 --> 11:32.000
Now was that a porpoise?

11:32.000 --> 11:36.000
I think so, I think it was a younger porpoise.

11:38.000 --> 11:42.000
Of course they were always on their own.

11:42.000 --> 11:48.000
You see you get them especially around about the island there, [Devar?/] Island.

11:49.000 --> 11:54.000
And then you might not see one again till you’re on the Isle of Ross.

11:55.000 --> 12:00.000
They’re never in twos and threes

12:00.000 --> 12:04.000
as you see with the porpoises.

12:05.000 --> 12:07.000
There’s half a dozen at a time.

12:08.000 --> 12:11.000
Now, we were speaking about shellfish.

12:11.000 --> 12:13.000
There are some specific names for shellfish.

12:13.000 --> 12:17.000
Can you remember any of these … local names?

12:22.000 --> 12:25.000
Spute fish - is that …?

12:25.000 --> 12:28.000
I think that's just spout fish.

12:29.000 --> 12:31.000
There are one or two here.

12:31.000 --> 12:33.000
The clappy dubh, are you familiar with that?

12:33.000 --> 12:38.000
Aye, that’s a bigger mussel, the cloopy dubh.

12:40.000 --> 12:42.000
You got them doon there about Achnan[XX].

12:43.000 --> 12:45.000
Used to use them for bait.

12:45.000 --> 12:46.000
Baiting lines?

12:46.000 --> 12:47.000
Aye.

12:48.000 --> 12:50.000
Would you gather them or dredge them up?

12:50.000 --> 12:51.000
Eh?

12:51.000 --> 12:54.000
Would you dredge them up or would you gather them by hand?

12:54.000 --> 12:56.000
No, you dredged them up.

12:57.000 --> 13:03.000
I mean you’re working out in maybe ten or a
dozen fathom of water [before you could get them/?].

13:08.000 --> 13:10.000
Did you ever hear of [saul dubh/?]?

13:10.000 --> 13:12.000
[Saul dubh/?], aye.

13:12.000 --> 13:14.000
What was that?

13:14.000 --> 13:17.000
Well, that's feeding for the fish.

13:18.000 --> 13:21.000
Did you have any idea what that meant? Did anybody ever … 

13:22.000 --> 13:23.000
[Saul dubh?/]?

13:24.000 --> 13:26.000
Aye … can you give an explanation of that?

13:37.000 --> 13:41.000
I think it was always [saul dubh/?] I heard them referred to.

13:41.000 --> 13:43.000
It’s big something isn’t it?

13:43.000 --> 13:46.000
Eh, well the …

13:47.000 --> 13:48.000
[Saul dubh/?]

13:48.000 --> 13:50.000
Well, dubh is black.

13:50.000 --> 13:52.000
Oh, black, aye.

13:53.000 --> 13:57.000
The Tarbert ones say it’s black eyes but the

13:57.000 --> 14:02.000
meaning you would expect would be black seed, like sile, seed.

14:03.000 --> 14:06.000
I think it possibly comes from the same word.

14:06.000 --> 14:08.000
You’re familiar with sile too?

14:08.000 --> 14:09.000
Aye, aye.

14:09.000 --> 14:13.000
What … what did that eh …?

14:13.000 --> 14:14.000
It’s young herring.

14:14.000 --> 14:15.000
Young herring.

14:15.000 --> 14:16.000
A-ha.

14:16.000 --> 14:20.000
Was there any other young fish that called by that name?

14:21.000 --> 14:23.000
That was called by that name?

14:23.000 --> 14:26.000
Not that I know of, no.

14:28.000 --> 14:30.000
Now, there’s two or three here.

14:33.000 --> 14:38.000
Now the winkie was called a winkie here, but

14:38.000 --> 14:41.000
was there any other name for it you heard of?

14:41.000 --> 14:43.000
Aye, when I went to the fishing at first.

14:43.000 --> 14:44.000
Do you take sugar?

14:44.000 --> 14:45.000
Eh, aye please.

14:45.000 --> 14:47.000
How much? Two?

14:47.000 --> 14:48.000
Is it a wee cup?

14:48.000 --> 14:50.000
Aye, they’re no very big.

14:50.000 --> 14:51.000
Eh, could I have one please.

14:51.000 --> 14:52.000
One? Just one?

14:52.000 --> 14:54.000
One will be fine thanks.

14:54.000 --> 14:55.000
We’re just about finished, en. [XX]

14:57.000 --> 15:01.000
When I went fishing first it was the [learie/?].

15:01.000 --> 15:03.000
The [learie/?]?

15:07.000 --> 15:08.000
You didna hear of stuthaidh?

15:08.000 --> 15:10.000
Stuthaidh - aye.

15:10.000 --> 15:11.000
That wasn’t …

15:11.000 --> 15:13.000
No they didn’t call it that.

15:13.000 --> 15:18.000
That was put on to the lines. Aye.

15:20.000 --> 15:23.000
Did they use that in Campbeltown,

15:23.000 --> 15:26.000
Stuthaidh. That word?

15:26.000 --> 15:30.000
Och aye, you’d hear an odd fella refer to it as stuthaidh.

15:32.000 --> 15:34.000
It wasn’t in general use you know.

15:35.000 --> 15:36.000
Was that just a buoy?

15:36.000 --> 15:39.000
It was a buoy with corks on it and maybe

15:39.000 --> 15:41.000
if you were on the lines, there were a flag on it, you see.

15:41.000 --> 15:42.000
That’s quite interesting.

15:42.000 --> 15:43.000
Eh?

15:43.000 --> 15:45.000
That’s quite interesting.

15:46.000 --> 15:50.000
I wonder if these men that were using that word were Gaelic speakers?

15:50.000 --> 15:52.000
Or came off a Gaelic … 

15:52.000 --> 15:53.000
Och no, I don't think so.

15:53.000 --> 15:54.000
Can you remember any of the men?

15:54.000 --> 15:59.000
They might eventually … no, no.

16:01.000 --> 16:03.000
What was that you said there?

16:03.000 --> 16:04.000
Eh, stuthaidh.

16:04.000 --> 16:05.000
Aye.

16:06.000 --> 16:10.000
No, I don’t think any of them’s Gaelic speakers.

16:10.000 --> 16:13.000
Now there’s another word eh … 

16:14.000 --> 16:16.000
was used for the,

16:16.000 --> 16:20.000
for a boat hook … it was a,

16:20.000 --> 16:25.000
a pole with a wooden cross [piece on the end/?].

16:26.000 --> 16:29.000
What was the name for that?

16:30.000 --> 16:32.000
Was that the …?

16:32.000 --> 16:33.000
Skiddaw.

16:34.000 --> 16:35.000
Skiddaw.

16:36.000 --> 16:40.000
Now, was that the only device which was called with that name?

16:40.000 --> 16:41.000
Was there anything else called that name?

16:41.000 --> 16:42.000
No. I can’t think.

16:42.000 --> 16:46.000
No, the skiddaw … that’s what you went for, the …

16:47.000 --> 16:49.000
There you are. Are you right?

16:49.000 --> 16:50.000
Thank you.

16:50.000 --> 16:54.000
I didn’t know whether to feed you up. [XX]

16:54.000 --> 16:57.000
There’s plenty mair, [Annie/?], aren’t there.

16:57.000 --> 17:02.000
[Dods/?] usually takes two, sometimes three. [XX] …

17:14.000 --> 17:17.000
Do you know the word [sgeochal/?]?

17:17.000 --> 17:19.000
[Sgeochal/?]?

17:19.000 --> 17:20.000
Aye, sgeochal.

17:24.000 --> 17:26.000
Here you are. [Have a wee biscuit/?]

17:27.000 --> 17:28.000
One will be fine. Thank you.

17:29.000 --> 17:31.000
No, [sgeochal/?].

17:31.000 --> 17:38.000
Oh, God [XX] … my feet’s … aye in the road.

17:38.000 --> 17:39.000
[Sgeochal/?], let me think.

17:39.000 --> 17:41.000
Margaret, have you heard that … 

17:41.000 --> 17:42.000
What’s that?

17:42.000 --> 17:43.000
The word [sgeochal/?]?

17:43.000 --> 17:44.000
[Sgeochal/?] [XX]

17:46.000 --> 17:49.000
Someone [sgeochaling/?], clearing his throat.

17:50.000 --> 17:52.000
Now there’s a name for that now.

17:54.000 --> 17:58.000
No, it’s not [sgeochal/?].

17:59.000 --> 18:02.000
No,  I think there’s a name for that right enough.

18:04.000 --> 18:05.000
[Did you/?] feed enough?

18:05.000 --> 18:06.000
I’m fine aye.

18:10.000 --> 18:12.000
[Neilogan/?] 

18:12.000 --> 18:13.000
What?

18:13.000 --> 18:14.000
[Neilogan/?]

18:18.000 --> 18:22.000
No, it’s a word I’ve never heard used very much.

18:22.000 --> 18:26.000
I believe I’ve heard it right enough I think and I canna,

18:26.000 --> 18:29.000
I canna think what it’s referred to at all.

18:31.000 --> 18:34.000
It’s just a … 

18:34.000 --> 18:37.000
a word I’ve only heard the one person [speaking about/?].

18:37.000 --> 18:41.000
I don’t, I don’t even know if it’s Gaelic or not.

18:41.000 --> 18:42.000
I’m just wondering.

18:42.000 --> 18:43.000
No.

18:43.000 --> 18:45.000
Och, it’s Gaelic [XX]- if it is a word.

18:46.000 --> 18:47.000
Gaelic.

18:54.000 --> 18:59.000
[XX] Just, just the final two words here.

19:00.000 --> 19:05.000
They both … meant a light breeze of wind.

19:07.000 --> 19:08.000
Eh?

19:08.000 --> 19:10.000
A light breeze of wind.

19:10.000 --> 19:13.000
Can you remember any words for a light breeze of wind?

19:14.000 --> 19:15.000
[Just an air/?]

19:15.000 --> 19:17.000
Did you hear of a sar?

19:17.000 --> 19:18.000
Sar? Aye.

19:18.000 --> 19:21.000
Sar, aye, sar. [...]

19:21.000 --> 19:28.000
An air of wind and a sar of wind.

19:29.000 --> 19:31.000
Was there any difference between the two?

19:31.000 --> 19:32.000
An air and a sar.

19:32.000 --> 19:35.000
They were just the same thing.

19:35.000 --> 19:37.000
Just a light, a light breeze?

19:37.000 --> 19:38.000
Aye, a light breeze.

19:38.000 --> 19:39.000
Very, very light.

19:39.000 --> 19:42.000
Now there’s a word up in Tarbert, flan.

19:42.000 --> 19:44.000
That’s the same.

19:45.000 --> 19:47.000
Do they use that in Campbeltown too?

19:47.000 --> 19:51.000
Hmm, yes, just a flan.

19:52.000 --> 19:53.000
That’s fine.

19:55.000 --> 19:58.000
God, did you never hear tell of burgoo?

19:58.000 --> 20:03.000
No, no, no, no. What does it mean?

20:03.000 --> 20:04.000
Porridge!

20:04.000 --> 20:05.000
Porridge!

20:05.000 --> 20:06.000
Aye.

20:06.000 --> 20:08.000
I never heard that, no.

20:09.000 --> 20:12.000
There was a man that worked at [Strathend/?] told me that.

20:12.000 --> 20:16.000
He worked on a farm. Galbraith, he was Davy Galbraith.

20:17.000 --> 20:21.000
[We just met in the café/?] and I was asking him if there,

20:21.000 --> 20:23.000
if there was a country word for [bringing in water/?].

20:23.000 --> 20:24.000
Aha.

20:24.000 --> 20:26.000
Did you ever … 

20:26.000 --> 20:30.000
are you familiar with any word used in 
the country farms for [drawing/?] in water?

20:30.000 --> 20:32.000
No, I was only a wee lassie.

20:32.000 --> 20:35.000
I don’t think I was five or so.

20:35.000 --> 20:36.000
Oh well.

20:36.000 --> 20:37.000
I wouldn’t know.

20:37.000 --> 20:38.000
Then we came into the town.

20:39.000 --> 20:42.000
What connection did you hear burgoo?

20:42.000 --> 20:44.000
It’s because that’s …

20:44.000 --> 20:50.000
many a time I heard porridge being referred to as burgoo.

20:51.000 --> 20:53.000
Many a time.

20:53.000 --> 20:55.000
Aboard the, the boats?

20:55.000 --> 20:57.000
Aye, and in the hoose.

20:57.000 --> 20:58.000
In the hoose, aye.

20:58.000 --> 21:03.000
Remember they used to [take/?] porridge [or oats/?]
or what was they had in the countryside in olden times.

21:03.000 --> 21:06.000
The pot used to go on the table

21:06.000 --> 21:08.000
and they would all [spoon/?] it out the pot.

21:08.000 --> 21:09.000
What was that? 

21:09.000 --> 21:10.000
Was it soup or porridge?

21:10.000 --> 21:14.000
It was porridge as far as I knew, or burgoo if you like. 

21:14.000 --> 21:16.000
All sitting round the table with a bit pot in the middle of the table

21:16.000 --> 21:19.000
and just taking spoonfuls out of the pot, you know.

21:21.000 --> 21:22.000
Some life!

21:22.000 --> 21:24.000
No, that was …

21:24.000 --> 21:25.000
No.

21:25.000 --> 21:29.000
I think they did all their cooking over an open fire too.

21:29.000 --> 21:31.000
That’s right, over an open fire.

21:31.000 --> 21:32.000
That’s right, aye.

21:32.000 --> 21:35.000
What was the arrangement for cooking?

21:35.000 --> 21:38.000
I don’t know how they, how they …

21:38.000 --> 21:42.000
Of course we should know that-[XX] my mother …

21:42.000 --> 21:44.000
it was an open fire my mother had.

21:44.000 --> 21:46.000
You see when the fire was open

21:46.000 --> 21:51.000
they had a thing, a ring, solid ring …

21:52.000 --> 21:54.000
That was the ranges.

21:54.000 --> 21:56.000
This was an open fire before that.

21:56.000 --> 21:58.000
Aye, well with an open fire too.

21:58.000 --> 22:02.000
You just put it [into an open/?] [XX]

22:02.000 --> 22:07.000
alongside the edge of the fire and it swung right oot on the top of the fire.

22:08.000 --> 22:10.000
You could swirl it right round …

22:13.000 --> 22:17.000
and you put your pot on the top of the [sling/?] and let it boil away.

22:17.000 --> 22:19.000
There was a name.

22:19.000 --> 22:20.000
What did you ca’ that?

22:21.000 --> 22:24.000
Well I know when we had the open fire there were two bits,

22:24.000 --> 22:27.000
one on each side of the fire, the big fire in the middle.

22:27.000 --> 22:31.000
You just put your pot at the side of the fire and […].

22:32.000 --> 22:34.000
Or if you were [any/?] in a hurry you put it on top of the fire.

22:34.000 --> 22:39.000
And you had a bar up, well up, up the chimney

22:42.000 --> 22:45.000
and you had a chain hooked on to that bar,

22:45.000 --> 22:50.000
with a hook in the bottom of the chain
and that hooked on to the handle of the pot.

22:51.000 --> 22:53.000
Now there was a name in that bar.

22:53.000 --> 22:55.000
Can you remember it?

22:55.000 --> 22:57.000
Aye, I canna mind about that wee thing.

22:57.000 --> 22:58.000
The [swee/?]?

22:58.000 --> 22:59.000
The [swee/?]?

23:01.000 --> 23:03.000
It might have been that thing, that thing that you out the pots on,

23:05.000 --> 23:08.000
you know that that wee twisted round [bit/?] … 

23:09.000 --> 23:10.000
I can’t remember.

23:11.000 --> 23:14.000
And then the gas rings came in.

23:16.000 --> 23:17.000
[XX]

23:17.000 --> 23:19.000
It we was very … 

23:23.000 --> 23:26.000
Now … 

23:26.000 --> 23:28.000
you didna … 

23:28.000 --> 23:31.000
cut peats at all? Was it coal?

23:31.000 --> 23:34.000
No, it was coal we burned really.

23:34.000 --> 23:35.000
We didn’t get …

23:35.000 --> 23:39.000
there weren’t many trees, you see. We never got … logs.

23:40.000 --> 23:42.000
Well, I’ll tell you what,

23:42.000 --> 23:46.000
they used to go over with the boat, over to Auchterferry

23:46.000 --> 23:50.000
and there was great wood came in there on the shore

23:50.000 --> 23:53.000
and they used to fill the boat with that and take it home

23:53.000 --> 23:56.000
and saw it up and we used that.

23:56.000 --> 24:00.000
And then there were whins at [Gruan na Creig/?] and in the [Garbost/?]

24:00.000 --> 24:02.000
and the [Barr/?]

24:02.000 --> 24:05.000
so sometimes they were as thick as that,

24:05.000 --> 24:06.000
the whins so they were beautiful.

24:06.000 --> 24:09.000
We used to burn them you see and then they were ready for …

24:09.000 --> 24:12.000
you burned them when they got withered

24:12.000 --> 24:17.000
and they were ready for to going in and you kindled your fire with that.

24:17.000 --> 24:18.000
My mother used to bake,

24:18.000 --> 24:22.000
with those whins you know - they made a lovely fire for baking.

24:22.000 --> 24:25.000
That would be the actual stems you would be using?

24:25.000 --> 24:26.000
Yes, that’s right.

24:27.000 --> 24:30.000
Oh, the jaggy bits were terrible. We left them.

24:31.000 --> 24:35.000
Would you just cut these with an axe or …

24:35.000 --> 24:36.000
Yes, they were very easy broken.

24:36.000 --> 24:39.000
Sometimes you broke them with your knees just or broke them with your hand.

24:39.000 --> 24:42.000
But of course if there were thicker ones you cut them with an axe.

24:42.000 --> 24:46.000
There was always plenty of instruments like that about the place.

24:46.000 --> 24:49.000
Would that be the … the job of the children?

24:50.000 --> 24:51.000
Pardon?

24:51.000 --> 24:52.000
Would it [have been/?] the children’s job?

24:52.000 --> 24:55.000
Yes, yes. We used to always go.

24:56.000 --> 25:00.000
And then the burning of the heather … the heather when it was burned -

25:01.000 --> 25:04.000
they called it in the Gaelic falaisg.  

25:04.000 --> 25:05.000
Falaisg?

25:05.000 --> 25:08.000
And it was very thin of course,

25:08.000 --> 25:11.000
wee thin bits but oh it was lovely for kindling a fire.

25:11.000 --> 25:13.000
We never needed to be without it.

25:13.000 --> 25:17.000
There was a peat moss up at the end of the Craig.

25:17.000 --> 25:20.000
They called it peat moss but it was under water.

25:20.000 --> 25:23.000
But there must have been peat dug there once upon a time

25:23.000 --> 25:26.000
because it was quite peaty at the side.

25:27.000 --> 25:32.000
But I don’t mind of our folk ever cutting peat really.

25:32.000 --> 25:33.000
Did they ever …?

25:33.000 --> 25:35.000
You know that gentleman I was on about.

25:35.000 --> 25:36.000
Well he said that,

25:36.000 --> 25:38.000
you know [XX] down the road,

25:38.000 --> 25:40.000
down [XX] Street …

25:40.000 --> 25:41.000
there’s a place there.

25:41.000 --> 25:44.000
Well he said that was the Gaelic word for whelks. 

25:44.000 --> 25:46.000
Oh, I see.

25:46.000 --> 25:48.000
[XX] That was what he said.

25:48.000 --> 25:50.000
Well, might be right enough.

25:50.000 --> 25:52.000
And they used to go down and fill their boats down there

25:52.000 --> 25:59.000
and then that paid the rent for the year or what ever way they wanted to do these…

26:00.000 --> 26:02.000
[XX] Well he said … that’s what he told me,

26:02.000 --> 26:04.000
that [peacach/?] was the Gaelic word for [whelks/?].

26:04.000 --> 26:06.000
Whether that’s true, I don't know.

26:07.000 --> 26:10.000
Sgadan [naturally/?] of course that’s the Gaelic for herring.

26:11.000 --> 26:14.000
But what do they call the rest of the fish I don’t know.

26:15.000 --> 26:19.000
Mackerel and whitings and haddocks and …?

26:19.000 --> 26:22.000
Reannach, in Tarbert anyway, reannach, mackerel.

26:22.000 --> 26:23.000
Mackerel.

26:23.000 --> 26:26.000
Well, a gobag was a dogfish.

26:26.000 --> 26:29.000
A dogfish, it was a dogfish right enough.

26:29.000 --> 26:31.000
And a mùrlach too.

26:31.000 --> 26:33.000
And a piocach, the piocach of course,

26:33.000 --> 26:36.000
the piocach was the, the saithe.

26:33.000 --> 26:37.000
The saithe. Aye, that’s right.

26:37.000 --> 26:39.000
That seems to be the word they use it Tarbert.

26:39.000 --> 26:42.000
But doon this way, it’s more glaisean.

26:42.000 --> 26:43.000
Aye, glaisean aye.

26:43.000 --> 26:46.000
The glaisean yes, that’s right, yes.

26:47.000 --> 26:50.000
We call the glaisean, did we call …?

26:50.000 --> 26:52.000
No, we called it [stemlach/?]. But was that the glaisean.

26:52.000 --> 26:53.000
I think that was the big ones

26:53.000 --> 26:55.000
That was the big ones, aye. The big ones.

26:55.000 --> 26:56.000
They’re lovely, salted.

26:59.000 --> 27:03.000
These … these saithe you salted, would they …

27:03.000 --> 27:05.000
would they be caught by hand lines?

27:05.000 --> 27:06.000
The saithe.

27:08.000 --> 27:09.000
No, no the rod.

27:09.000 --> 27:10.000
The rods.

27:10.000 --> 27:11.000
We used the rods.

27:11.000 --> 27:14.000
Would that just be from the rocks?

27:14.000 --> 27:17.000
You were out on the loch and you rowed all the time,

27:17.000 --> 27:19.000
and you drew it after you, you see,

27:19.000 --> 27:20.000
and then you caught them.

27:20.000 --> 27:23.000
And oh what a job it used to be taking them in.

27:23.000 --> 27:27.000
We used to … we weren’t good at taking them in, that’s us as young folk.

27:27.000 --> 27:29.000
We used to swing them round our heads, you know,

27:29.000 --> 27:31.000
and hitting somebody in the face.

27:32.000 --> 27:36.000
You had a board across the stern, you see, with a slot.

27:36.000 --> 27:38.000
You know, two boards nailed with a slot

27:38.000 --> 27:40.000
and the rod went in there, spread out.

27:40.000 --> 27:43.000
That was a [sgathach/?], wasn’t it?

27:43.000 --> 27:44.000
That’s right, yes.

27:44.000 --> 27:46.000
That was a [sgathach/?] they called that boat

27:47.000 --> 27:50.000
and you didn’t get them just till the [dusk/?] came in

27:50.000 --> 27:53.000
and then you wouldn’t get one and then they would just come up

27:53.000 --> 27:55.000
when the [dusk/?] came in. You could follow them …

27:55.000 --> 27:58.000
Yes, they just rowed very slowly through the [XX].

27:58.000 --> 27:59.000
[XX] and then take them in.

27:59.000 --> 28:00.000
Yes.

28:04.000 --> 28:09.000
You mentioned too that the fishermen used to go for coals.

28:09.000 --> 28:12.000
Yes [XX]

28:13.000 --> 28:15.000
they went to Greenock for coal

28:15.000 --> 28:17.000
with a fishing [skift/?]

28:17.000 --> 28:21.000
and they took so many tonne- I don't know how many tonne it would be.

28:22.000 --> 28:25.000
And then of course they couldn’t,

28:25.000 --> 28:28.000
of course they couldn’t get the fishing [skift/?] in

28:28.000 --> 28:32.000
near the shore because it ebbed up a good bit.

28:32.000 --> 28:36.000
You know, the ebb went out and …

28:36.000 --> 28:39.000
but when it was high water and that they took it in pretty near

28:39.000 --> 28:43.000
and then of course they just carried it in bags or in a barra,

28:43.000 --> 28:45.000
you know, into the place.

28:45.000 --> 28:49.000
Of course it was never put indoors - it was just left in a place.

28:49.000 --> 28:51.000
We called it the cùlag,

28:51.000 --> 28:53.000
I don’t know what that means really. 

28:53.000 --> 28:54.000
It’s a Gaelic name

28:54.000 --> 28:59.000
but it was this place at the byre and you just emptied it in there.

28:59.000 --> 29:01.000
And the coal was beautiful …

29:01.000 --> 29:03.000
oh it was so cheap too

29:03.000 --> 29:05.000
and it was glossy, thon glossy coal.

29:13.000 --> 29:15.000
We had a well. The well was beautiful.

29:15.000 --> 29:18.000
It was as cold in summer as it was in the winter,

29:18.000 --> 29:22.000
with the spring just coming from the hill.

29:22.000 --> 29:24.000
And we cleaned that well-

29:24.000 --> 29:26.000
we had to clean it regular

29:26.000 --> 29:29.000
and we put those white chuckie stones, as they call it,

29:29.000 --> 29:31.000
in the bottom of the well

29:31.000 --> 29:35.000
and we would catch a trout in the burn and put it in to keep it clean.

29:35.000 --> 29:38.000
It would eat up all the weeds and things.

29:38.000 --> 29:39.000
That’s what we did.

29:39.000 --> 29:40.000
That trout would be there all year?

29:40.000 --> 29:43.000
It went round all the time, it lived there all the time.

29:43.000 --> 29:45.000
It was wonderful really.

29:46.000 --> 29:51.000
And then there was always a wee outlet for the water to go very slowly,

29:51.000 --> 29:54.000
you know, in case the water might get sort of stagnant.

29:54.000 --> 29:56.000
But it never did of course.

29:56.000 --> 30:00.000
Was that well actually … would it be … was it bricked?

30:00.000 --> 30:04.000
No, it was just … it just seemed to be stones right around it.

30:04.000 --> 30:09.000
[There were/?] some flagstones at the side, you bent down and then you carried …

30:09.000 --> 30:11.000
it was stoups you had. 

30:11.000 --> 30:12.000
Did you hear of the stoups?

30:12.000 --> 30:14.000
Stoup? Stoup? [Oh aye/?]

30:14.000 --> 30:20.000
They’re in Auchindrain - they’re in Auchindrain Museum I think our stoups,

30:21.000 --> 30:24.000
at [Silvercraig/?] I think Miss Campbell [Kilberry/?] came down

30:24.000 --> 30:27.000
so I think May gave her the stoups.

30:27.000 --> 30:30.000
It was a wooden … they made the stoups too of course, the men.

30:30.000 --> 30:34.000
It was wooden … it was just round … they were round and wooden.

30:34.000 --> 30:37.000
They were that size

30:37.000 --> 30:38.000
and then there was just a handle.

30:38.000 --> 30:40.000
The handle was fitted into each side.

30:41.000 --> 30:44.000
But my goodness when you went to carry you had to take two

30:44.000 --> 30:48.000
to balance yourself coming from the water.

30:49.000 --> 30:52.000
Oh, it was, it was really great.

30:52.000 --> 30:54.000
That was the stoups.

31:09.000 --> 31:13.000
Now I asked you about the word for wood shavings.

31:22.000 --> 31:23.000
… Sliseags?

31:26.000 --> 31:31.000
That was used only in connection with kindling the fire?

31:31.000 --> 31:36.000
[...]

31:36.000 --> 31:42.000
and you broke it off into sliseags for starting the fire.

31:42.000 --> 31:45.000
That was the only use of the word?

31:45.000 --> 31:51.000
Aye, that was all […], sliseags.

31:51.000 --> 31:56.000
Now, you mentioned [screenge/?] net too.

31:58.000 --> 31:59.000
[Screenge/?]?

31:59.000 --> 32:00.000
Was that an old one?

32:00.000 --> 32:03.000
No, well no, no.

32:03.000 --> 32:11.000
That was when they started with those ground nets for [lèabag/?],

32:11.000 --> 32:12.000
for flounders.

32:12.000 --> 32:16.000
They called it a screenge.

32:16.000 --> 32:18.000
That was later days.

32:18.000 --> 32:25.000
A screenge net - it originally came from the East Coast.

32:26.000 --> 32:28.000
Just a ground net.

32:28.000 --> 32:29.000
That’s interesting …

32:29.000 --> 32:33.000
I’ve come across the word in connection with the West Highlands.

32:33.000 --> 32:37.000
It was used for a wee trawl net, a wee ring net.

32:37.000 --> 32:39.000
They used to haul them to the shore.

32:41.000 --> 32:45.000
But eh … that seems to be a … a recent …

32:45.000 --> 32:47.000
Screenge is a different thing a’ the gether.

32:48.000 --> 32:50.000
But a [ballicrubach/?]

32:51.000 --> 32:53.000
I was going to come to that word. Aye …

32:55.000 --> 32:57.000
The [wee/?] net.

32:57.000 --> 33:02.000
You know, they [would get tied up/?], you know.

33:02.000 --> 33:06.000
A [XX] [newton/?] would tie up the torn butt

33:06.000 --> 33:12.000
with the [XX] butt [XX] [crubble/?] net

33:12.000 --> 33:13.000
Cupple net?

33:13.000 --> 33:14.000
Crubble net.

33:14.000 --> 33:16.000
[Crùbach/?] [XX]

33:17.000 --> 33:23.000
Whatever way they joined the two [XX] a [ballicrubach/?].

33:24.000 --> 33:27.000
Very [friendly/?]  Gaelic, very old Gaelic.

33:29.000 --> 33:31.000
That’s what [they did/?], a [ballicrubach/?].

33:31.000 --> 33:38.000
Did [everybody/?] apply that word to just a wee ring net reduced in size?

33:38.000 --> 33:41.000
When they were [teeing/?] the net

33:41.000 --> 33:43.000
we would telling them to make a [ballicrubach/?].

33:44.000 --> 33:46.000
And they would work away at it.

33:46.000 --> 33:47.000
So that was the application of the word?

33:47.000 --> 33:50.000
Aye [...]

33:50.000 --> 33:53.000
Now … I asked you before …

33:53.000 --> 34:00.000
Well, but [...] tied up [the torn/?] [XX] [ballicrubach/?].

34:05.000 --> 34:09.000
Was the word sùgan ever used in that context? [XX] 

34:09.000 --> 34:10.000
Sùgan? 

34:10.000 --> 34:11.000
Sùgan

34:11.000 --> 34:13.000
Och, aye. They aye used the sùgan

34:13.000 --> 34:16.000
when they got a net fairly heavy.

34:16.000 --> 34:19.000
They made a bunch of the net,

34:19.000 --> 34:25.000
a sùgan and tied it to [it/?] with a rope on to the [gunnel/?].

34:25.000 --> 34:27.000
That was keeping the whole [of the weight/?] away from the herring.

34:27.000 --> 34:29.000
That was a sùgan.

34:29.000 --> 34:32.000
That’s the only use that word had?

34:32.000 --> 34:36.000
Aye. That’s the only use it was [after/?], sùgan just,

34:36.000 --> 34:37.000
keeping the herring up.

34:41.000 --> 34:43.000
That was tied, the boat was [XX],

34:43.000 --> 34:47.000
the rest was heaving up against that sùgan.

34:48.000 --> 34:53.000
You see that’s for sticking up [XX] a big bunch [XX] of leaves in the net,

34:53.000 --> 34:55.000
tied round with a rope

34:55.000 --> 34:57.000
and the last of the stringer

34:57.000 --> 35:00.000
and then they would drag the herring up to the…

35:00.000 --> 35:03.000
that was never touched until the net was empty.

35:03.000 --> 35:14.000
Sùgan. [...] That was the sùgan.

35:14.000 --> 35:16.000
Now there’s an expression here …

35:18.000 --> 35:19.000
I read …

35:24.000 --> 35:27.000
I think I read of this in connection with Loch Fyne fishing.

35:28.000 --> 35:33.000
I don’t know whether you’re familiar with it - [salachar iasg/?],

35:36.000 --> 35:38.000
[salachar iasg/?] -

35:38.000 --> 35:43.000
the herring [dirt/?] I think would be the definition.

35:43.000 --> 35:45.000
Did you ever hear of that? …

35:48.000 --> 35:49.000
No?

35:50.000 --> 35:52.000
What did you call it again?

35:52.000 --> 35:57.000
[Salachar iasg/?], [salachar iasg/?]

36:01.000 --> 36:04.000
No, I never heard about that.

36:04.000 --> 36:05.000
It was aye the herring [dirt/?].

36:05.000 --> 36:07.000
Just the [dirt/?] they called it?

36:07.000 --> 36:08.000
The herring [dirt/?].

36:10.000 --> 36:12.000
You would get it together on the tides.

36:13.000 --> 36:16.000
No, I never heard that expression.

36:16.000 --> 36:17.000
Now …

36:18.000 --> 36:21.000
Of course, old men might use it. [XX] 

36:21.000 --> 36:27.000
This may have been up about Minard and towards the north.

36:27.000 --> 36:29.000
