I’ve heard you saying it.

01:20:15.000 --> 01:20:16.000
[Balugran/?]? No.

01:20:20.000 --> 01:20:23.000
[Balugran/?]. What about sùgan?

01:20:23.000 --> 01:20:25.000
I’ve heard o’ that.

01:20:25.000 --> 01:20:27.000
[Suigean/?]? That’s a plant …

01:20:27.000 --> 01:20:29.000
sùgans.

01:20:31.000 --> 01:20:33.000
You’re no’ thinking of [summons/?], no?

01:20:34.000 --> 01:20:37.000
I thought it was …

01:20:37.000 --> 01:20:40.000
when we were going on a picnic years ago …

01:20:40.000 --> 01:20:41.000
before I was married,

01:20:41.000 --> 01:20:46.000
Angus was there and somebody was saying ‘Oh, it’s going to rain,

01:20:46.000 --> 01:20:48.000
it’s …’, ‘Och’ they said ‘You’re a sùgan’,

01:20:48.000 --> 01:20:50.000
meaning you were putting a dampener on everything.

01:20:50.000 --> 01:20:51.000
I’ve never heard that.

01:20:51.000 --> 01:20:52.000
Well, that’s …

01:20:54.000 --> 01:20:59.000
I thought really it was a plant, like a [sùrag/?].

01:21:00.000 --> 01:21:02.000
You didn’t hear of a [gadrach/?]?

01:21:02.000 --> 01:21:03.000
No.

01:21:03.000 --> 01:21:06.000
[Gadrach/?]? Is that to gad aboot, no?

01:21:06.000 --> 01:21:09.000
No. It’s more a fishing [XX]. It just means mibbe …

01:21:12.000 --> 01:21:15.000
a [sleever/?], no?

01:21:15.000 --> 01:21:17.000
Aye, ‘I’ll gie you a sleever.’

01:21:16.000 --> 01:21:17.000
No, think of something else.

01:21:17.000 --> 01:21:18.000
I think that’s more English.

01:21:18.000 --> 01:21:21.000
Have you no’ heard of men having a sleever, a right good drink?

01:21:21.000 --> 01:21:22.000
No.

01:21:22.000 --> 01:21:24.000
A right sleever.

01:21:24.000 --> 01:21:25.000
I’ve heard of that.

01:21:25.000 --> 01:21:28.000
Oh, have you? No, I’ve never heard of that.

01:21:37.000 --> 01:21:40.000
Was there a local name for porridge?

01:21:40.000 --> 01:21:41.000
[Stourins/?]. 

01:21:42.000 --> 01:21:43.000
[Stourins/?]?

01:21:43.000 --> 01:21:45.000
[Stourins/?].

01:21:45.000 --> 01:21:46.000
Away!

01:21:48.000 --> 01:21:49.000
Where did you hear that?

01:21:49.000 --> 01:21:52.000
Well, when I …

01:21:52.000 --> 01:21:55.000
when we were in Gayfield Place and I was only a wee lassie

01:21:56.000 --> 01:21:59.000
Mrs Anderson, you know Barbara and Flora and them?

01:21:59.000 --> 01:22:01.000
Their mother was next door

01:22:01.000 --> 01:22:03.000
and she’d come oot and she’d shout

01:22:03.000 --> 01:22:05.000
‘Come in and get your [stourins/?].

01:22:05.000 --> 01:22:06.000
They’re lifted.’

01:22:06.000 --> 01:22:09.000
And sometimes I’d go in and it was a plate of porridge.

01:22:09.000 --> 01:22:12.000
But I never heard your granny say it,

01:22:12.000 --> 01:22:15.000
it was aye porridge but that was what she called it - [stourins/?].

01:22:15.000 --> 01:22:18.000
Must have been … used.

01:22:19.000 --> 01:22:20.000
I’ll clour you-

01:22:22.000 --> 01:22:24.000
wallop you but he never did it.

01:22:24.000 --> 01:22:28.000
What was the boat that Johnny had? [Clourin/?].

01:22:29.000 --> 01:22:31.000
Is that no’ a Gaelic name?

01:22:34.000 --> 01:22:37.000
Aye, she came from [Carradale/?]

01:22:37.000 --> 01:22:39.000
but I’m no’ just quite sure what that means.

01:22:39.000 --> 01:22:41.000
What, was it the boat that came from [Carradale/?]? 
Aye, I think so.

01:22:41.000 --> 01:22:44.000
Who had it? Was it Johnny? No.

01:22:44.000 --> 01:22:46.000
Had he the Ella? No.

01:22:46.000 --> 01:22:50.000
[Scaff/?]

01:22:51.000 --> 01:22:52.000
No.

01:22:55.000 --> 01:22:56.000
[XX] the word for a pig?

01:22:57.000 --> 01:22:58.000
[Duircean/?]

01:23:01.000 --> 01:23:03.000
That’s just what it meant.
You’ve got a good memory.

01:23:07.000 --> 01:23:08.000
You didn’t hear a word sàrach?

01:23:08.000 --> 01:23:09.000
Aye

01:23:10.000 --> 01:23:12.000
Aye, what’s that?
Wait a minute …

01:23:15.000 --> 01:23:16.000
sàrach.

01:23:17.000 --> 01:23:20.000
Would it mean raw? No?

01:23:21.000 --> 01:23:23.000
‘Tastes sarach’…

01:23:24.000 --> 01:23:25.000
I’ve heard the word.

01:23:25.000 --> 01:23:26.000
Sour, no?

01:23:27.000 --> 01:23:31.000
No, the usual connection it’s used in is maybe trouble.

01:23:31.000 --> 01:23:34.000
Fishermen use it a lot [when/?] it’s sàrach. 

01:23:34.000 --> 01:23:36.000
A bit of bother … a problem.

01:23:43.000 --> 01:23:46.000
Have you ever [heard/?] smùrach used in connection with the weather?

01:23:50.000 --> 01:23:51.000
No’ really.

01:23:51.000 --> 01:23:53.000
It’s smùrach the day.

01:23:54.000 --> 01:23:56.000
It could be right enough.

01:23:57.000 --> 01:23:58.000
A smùrach kind of day.

01:23:58.000 --> 01:24:01.000
Oh dear knows - I just canna mind.

01:24:02.000 --> 01:24:05.000
You never found out what [tories/?] meant?

01:24:05.000 --> 01:24:07.000
There was this old woman that had the Gaelic

01:24:07.000 --> 01:24:08.000
and she used to say to Bill and I,

01:24:08.000 --> 01:24:10.000
'Oh, my, you’re a pair of tories.’

01:24:10.000 --> 01:24:11.000
What it meant I don’t know.

01:24:11.000 --> 01:24:12.000
No, I havna’ [XX].

01:24:12.000 --> 01:24:18.000
Auntie Teenie used to say to Barbara ‘You wee [torie lough/?]’.

01:24:18.000 --> 01:24:21.000
Whatever that meant I don’t know.

01:24:21.000 --> 01:24:22.000
There’s a word … you mentioned there,

01:24:22.000 --> 01:24:23.000
Torie lough.

01:24:23.000 --> 01:24:24.000
[sgeochal/?]

01:24:24.000 --> 01:24:27.000
Aye, [sgeochal/?] [Sgeochal/?]?

01:24:27.000 --> 01:24:30.000
Aye, it’s to clear your throat.

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:31.000
Where did you get that word?

01:24:31.000 --> 01:24:32.000
Auntie Tina.

01:24:32.000 --> 01:24:36.000
She’d say ‘Stop your [sgeochling/?].’

01:24:36.000 --> 01:24:38.000
The likes o’ me coughing like that.

01:24:39.000 --> 01:24:41.000
I never heard o’ it.
Aye, she used to say that a lot.

01:24:41.000 --> 01:24:43.000
Did she have Gaelic?

01:24:43.000 --> 01:24:44.000
Aye, I don’t know.

01:24:44.000 --> 01:24:46.000
She came from up country but …

01:24:46.000 --> 01:24:47.000
and she would say tuirn,

01:24:47.000 --> 01:24:50.000
tuirneaps and rùbrab, rùbrab.

01:24:50.000 --> 01:24:53.000
Aye, Johhny’s wife aye said that too. 

01:24:53.000 --> 01:24:55.000
Is that the country?

01:24:55.000 --> 01:24:57.000
Well, I don’t know.

01:24:57.000 --> 01:25:00.000
Just, she used to say rùbrab,

01:25:00.000 --> 01:25:02.000
rùbrab and tuirneap.

01:25:02.000 --> 01:25:03.000
Tuirneap.

01:25:04.000 --> 01:25:06.000
Aye … she always said that.

01:25:09.000 --> 01:25:12.000
Now, you didna hear the word brash?

01:25:12.000 --> 01:25:14.000
Oh, aye brash. Bold? No, no. No?

01:25:15.000 --> 01:25:16.000
No, no.

01:25:16.000 --> 01:25:17.000
No.

01:25:17.000 --> 01:25:21.000
But, eh … rough and ready.

01:25:21.000 --> 01:25:22.000
You know, you’re awful brash just.

01:25:22.000 --> 01:25:24.000
No, this was [XX] …
Quick.

01:25:24.000 --> 01:25:28.000
Like a brash. [XX] say there was a brash.

01:25:28.000 --> 01:25:30.000
It was maybe more used at the fishing.

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:35.000
It just meant there was a sudden rush of [XX], you know. Now …

01:25:35.000 --> 01:25:37.000
Well, if you say anybody’s brash …

01:25:39.000 --> 01:25:42.000
you, if you were running out there:

01:25:42.000 --> 01:25:45.000
‘Oh, he’s that brash.’ I’ve heard that. That’s the …

01:25:45.000 --> 01:25:46.000
I think that’s English.

01:25:46.000 --> 01:25:50.000
Whether the word … whether this other word brash is English too.

01:25:51.000 --> 01:25:57.000
What about … was there a word moggans?

01:25:57.000 --> 01:25:58.000
Aye

01:25:58.000 --> 01:26:00.000
Aye, your moggans.

01:26:00.000 --> 01:26:03.000
Is it a moggan of … wine, beer?

01:26:03.000 --> 01:26:04.000
No, that’s noggin.

01:26:04.000 --> 01:26:06.000
Noggin, oh aye. Moggans.

01:26:06.000 --> 01:26:08.000
Is that no’ your shoes?
I used to say moggans for my wee shoes.

01:26:08.000 --> 01:26:09.000
Shoes.

01:26:09.000 --> 01:26:12.000
Wee [Amelia/?] used to say to my granny,

01:26:12.000 --> 01:26:14.000
‘Can you knit me moggans?’

01:26:14.000 --> 01:26:16.000
Aye, moggans. Wee [XX]. Wee shoes.

01:26:16.000 --> 01:26:19.000
I’m sure that MacLellan had the nickname ‘Moggans’. Moggans.

01:26:19.000 --> 01:26:20.000
Why was that?

01:26:20.000 --> 01:26:22.000
That was a fisherman, Moggans.

01:26:22.000 --> 01:26:27.000
Some o’ they MacLellans were related maybe to your grandfather, some of them.

01:26:27.000 --> 01:26:29.000
What did you understand the word to mean?

01:26:29.000 --> 01:26:33.000
I mean no … not used as a nickname, but in general.

01:26:34.000 --> 01:26:40.000
I knew that it referred to slippers but then again I thought there was a plant in it.

01:26:40.000 --> 01:26:43.000
Again, a wild plant.

01:26:43.000 --> 01:26:44.000
Maybe I’m getting mixed up.

01:26:44.000 --> 01:26:46.000
No, it’s slippers that I heard.
Oh, it’s slippers, aye.

01:26:46.000 --> 01:26:47.000
Oh that’s …

01:26:47.000 --> 01:26:50.000
I’m looking for that meaning.

01:26:50.000 --> 01:26:53.000
You didn’t hear of moggan in any other sense?

01:26:53.000 --> 01:26:54.000
No.

01:26:54.000 --> 01:26:55.000
To mog something. No.

01:26:57.000 --> 01:27:00.000
Are you familiar with the word [mùchaidh/?]?

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:02.000
[Mùchaidh/?]? Oh, aye.

01:27:02.000 --> 01:27:03.000
Oh, [mùchaidh/?], aye.

01:27:03.000 --> 01:27:05.000
A sort of [mùchaidh/?] day. A damp …

01:27:05.000 --> 01:27:06.000
Close. 

01:27:06.000 --> 01:27:08.000
Close, aye.

01:27:08.000 --> 01:27:13.000
Humid- that’s a better word.

01:27:13.000 --> 01:27:14.000
Aye.

01:27:18.000 --> 01:27:20.000
Roochlin, I don’t know but I think this is Scots.

01:27:20.000 --> 01:27:22.000
What do you take that to mean?

01:27:22.000 --> 01:27:24.000
‘Gie you a good roochlin.’

01:27:24.000 --> 01:27:27.000
Aye, a good shaking or what.

01:27:27.000 --> 01:27:30.000
Aye, rough and tumble, you know.

01:27:36.000 --> 01:27:39.000
What about clappy doo? What was that?

01:27:40.000 --> 01:27:42.000
Clappy doo’s a shellfish isn’t it?

01:27:44.000 --> 01:27:48.000
Were there any names, local names for shellfish you can remember?

01:27:48.000 --> 01:27:50.000
Shellfish that you’d eat?

01:27:51.000 --> 01:27:52.000
[Sput/?] fish.

01:27:52.000 --> 01:27:55.000
Aye, I think that’s just from spout.

01:27:55.000 --> 01:27:56.000
That was the razors.

01:27:56.000 --> 01:27:57.000
Razor fish.

01:27:57.000 --> 01:27:58.000
Razor fish.

01:28:01.000 --> 01:28:03.000
You didn’t hear the word [dralag/?]?

01:28:03.000 --> 01:28:04.000
[Dralag/?].

01:28:04.000 --> 01:28:12.000
I think that’s more a fisherman’s term too or [XX].

01:28:12.000 --> 01:28:14.000
I think that’s more up country.

01:28:16.000 --> 01:28:17.000
I think that’s about it.

01:28:26.000 --> 01:28:29.000
She was making, was it [mealan creesh/?]?

01:28:29.000 --> 01:28:30.000
[Mealan creesh/?]?

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:31.000
Aye. Mealie-creesh.

01:28:31.000 --> 01:28:32.000
What was that?

01:28:32.000 --> 01:28:35.000
[Pasty/?] pudding we called it and mealie-creesh.

01:28:35.000 --> 01:28:39.000
Was it no’ oatmeal, onion and something?

01:28:39.000 --> 01:28:41.000
You put in your dripping if you had roast dripping and it lovely,

01:28:41.000 --> 01:28:43.000
and chopped onions and then your meal a’ mixed up.

01:28:43.000 --> 01:28:44.000
Meal, aye.

01:28:44.000 --> 01:28:46.000
I believe that will be tasty.

01:28:46.000 --> 01:28:47.000
[It would make you eat/?].

01:28:47.000 --> 01:28:51.000
We used to aye make it. Duncan was awfa’ fond of it.

01:28:51.000 --> 01:28:52.000
Aye.

01:28:53.000 --> 01:28:57.000
His mother used to sing it to them when they were wee

01:28:57.000 --> 01:28:59.000
‘Fhir a’ bhàta na horò eile.

01:28:59.000 --> 01:29:02.000
I climb the mountains and scan the ocean

01:29:02.000 --> 01:29:05.000
for thee, dear boatman in fond devotion’.

01:29:05.000 --> 01:29:06.000
It’s a nice yin.

01:29:07.000 --> 01:29:09.000
‘When shall I see thee? Today, tomorrow?

01:29:09.000 --> 01:29:11.000
Oh, do not leave me in lonely sorrow’.

01:29:11.000 --> 01:29:12.000
It’s sad.

01:29:12.000 --> 01:29:15.000
And then they sing ‘Fhir a’ bhàta’ as the chorus.

01:29:15.000 --> 01:29:17.000
Oh, fhir a’ … the Gaelic, aye.

01:29:17.000 --> 01:29:18.000
Aye.

01:29:18.000 --> 01:29:21.000
Auntie Tina used to say to Barbara ‘You hoolet.’

01:29:21.000 --> 01:29:22.000
‘You hoolet.'

01:29:22.000 --> 01:29:24.000
That’s an owl, that’s Scots.

01:29:24.000 --> 01:29:25.000
Oh, that’s no Gaelic, no?

01:29:25.000 --> 01:29:27.000
No, that’s a Scots word.

01:29:27.000 --> 01:29:28.000
She used to [XX] like that.

01:29:28.000 --> 01:29:29.000
I never …

01:29:30.000 --> 01:29:33.000
when you’re young you don’t take in all these words, you know.
No, you don’t.

01:29:33.000 --> 01:29:35.000
It’s when you look back.

01:29:39.000 --> 01:29:40.000
I think that’s it.

01:29:43.000 --> 01:29:45.000
No, that’s fine …

01:29:47.000 --> 01:29:49.000
Och aye there’d be other words if you could just mind.

01:29:49.000 --> 01:29:51.000
What was that …

01:29:54.000 --> 01:29:56.000
was it [sarach/?]? [Sarach/?].

01:29:56.000 --> 01:29:58.000
Aye, a [sarach/?].

01:29:58.000 --> 01:30:01.000
I’m sure that’s something to do with the wind.

01:30:01.000 --> 01:30:02.000
[Sarach/?] [wind/?].

01:30:02.000 --> 01:30:03.000
A good [sarach/?] wind.

01:30:03.000 --> 01:30:07.000
I’m nearly sure that that was a saying.

01:30:08.000 --> 01:30:11.000
I think it would be a fresh wind, you know.

01:30:13.000 --> 01:30:15.000
I could be wrong … but

01:30:15.000 --> 01:30:17.000
I know the word, well I don’t know any other meaning.

01:30:20.000 --> 01:30:21.000
[Sarach/?]. Now [brouser/?]?

01:30:21.000 --> 01:30:22.000
[Brouser/?].

01:30:23.000 --> 01:30:24.000
Bouncer.

01:30:24.000 --> 01:30:26.000
Big bouncing boy.

01:30:26.000 --> 01:30:28.000
That’s what they used to say. He’s a [brouser/?].

01:30:28.000 --> 01:30:29.000
That’s what your dad used to …

01:30:29.000 --> 01:30:31.000
when your granny would be carrying him out in a plaid

01:30:31.000 --> 01:30:34.000
and this woman stayed on the high road.

01:30:34.000 --> 01:30:37.000
She’d say ‘Can I see the baby’s legs?’

01:30:37.000 --> 01:30:39.000
She’d to open the plaid or plaid.

01:30:39.000 --> 01:30:42.000
He was an awfa’ big wean and …

01:30:42.000 --> 01:30:46.000
and my mother said I’m sure he’d be twelve or twelve and a half pound.

01:30:46.000 --> 01:30:47.000
A big boy.

01:30:49.000 --> 01:30:51.000
That applied to a … baby.

01:30:53.000 --> 01:30:55.000
You didn’t hear the word a ‘mush’?

01:30:55.000 --> 01:31:00.000
Aye, mush. Aye, it’s a wild mush.

01:31:02.000 --> 01:31:04.000
It’s just what it is.

01:31:04.000 --> 01:31:07.000
Mix up. Like [misleach/?].

01:31:07.000 --> 01:31:11.000
A mush would be, to me, something kind of soft and mushy.

01:31:11.000 --> 01:31:16.000
No but I think this mix up is more a local term.

01:31:16.000 --> 01:31:18.000
They used it at the fishing- ‘wi a mush o’ boats.’

01:31:21.000 --> 01:31:25.000
Ochan ee. I was looking at a photograph there when I was looking for the car papers.

01:31:25.000 --> 01:31:28.000
There’s two or three boats in the Carradale at the quay,

01:31:28.000 --> 01:31:29.000
fishing boats you know.

01:31:31.000 --> 01:31:33.000
I don’t know who they were or whose they were. 

01:31:33.000 --> 01:31:35.000
That word you used there ‘ochan eh’ is that …

01:31:35.000 --> 01:31:36.000
Ochan ee

01:31:36.000 --> 01:31:38.000
Ochan ee an ee och ’s och ’s och.

01:31:38.000 --> 01:31:41.000
That must be … that must be a Gaelic word.

01:31:49.000 --> 01:31:51.000
That word [railleach/?]

01:31:51.000 --> 01:31:54.000
Is it kind of cold, no feeling.

01:31:54.000 --> 01:31:57.000
Who would that apply to?

01:31:58.000 --> 01:32:00.000
To anybody.

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:01.000
Listen, eh …

01:32:01.000 --> 01:32:04.000
oh, no you had that word that’s kind of like it.

01:32:06.000 --> 01:32:08.000
Now, that word [suna/?]

01:32:08.000 --> 01:32:11.000
[Suna/?]. Well, I don’t know how it’s spelt but

01:32:11.000 --> 01:32:13.000
I’ve heard it used often enough:

01:32:14.000 --> 01:32:18.000
‘A wee suna’, and if your dad was here he would mind it too.

01:32:21.000 --> 01:32:22.000
What was the other one?

01:32:24.000 --> 01:32:30.000
‘I’ll gie you a [lunering/?], a skelp or something.

01:32:30.000 --> 01:32:33.000
That was used, again, in childhood?

01:32:33.000 --> 01:32:37.000
Aye. Aye, I’ve heard him saying he got a wild lunering,

01:32:37.000 --> 01:32:40.000
so it must have meant that.

01:32:42.000 --> 01:32:43.000
But eh …

01:32:44.000 --> 01:32:51.000
[XX] she’s no’ that old and she could mind the … the names.

01:32:51.000 --> 01:32:54.000
‘A wee suna’, I don’t know what that would be.

01:32:54.000 --> 01:32:56.000
It’s no’ in any of your Gaelic …

01:32:57.000 --> 01:32:58.000
That’s fine.

01:33:01.000 --> 01:33:03.000
A scatterin’ o’ herring.

01:33:03.000 --> 01:33:05.000
‘Oh, there was just a scatterin’ in it’ if they were asked

01:33:05.000 --> 01:33:08.000
if there was much herring. ‘No, just a scaterrin.’

01:33:08.000 --> 01:33:11.000
I think that would be English right enough.